<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-1-4" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Slope and Rate of Change</title>

<assemblage component="instructor">
<p>This Lesson reviews the notion of slope, which students learned in elementary algebra, but emphasizes the meaning of the slope as a rate of change. Notice that in the beginning of this Lesson we do not use the coordinate formula for slope, <m>y=\dfrac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}</m>;  instead all of the slopes in the Lesson can be computed from the definition, <m>m=\dfrac{\Delta y}{\Delta x}</m>, and we emphasize that approach. Encourage students to include units in their calculation of <m>\Delta y</m> and <m>\Delta x</m> to help them interpret the slope as a rate. The two-point formula is introduced at the end of the textbook section, and will be used in the next section to introduce the point-slope formula for a line.</p>

<p>Two skills will probably be new to students:  deciding if data are linear by checking for constant slope, and using the slope to calculate either <m>\Delta y</m> or <m>\Delta x</m>, given a value for the other.</p>

<p>GOALS</p>
<p><ul>
    <li><p>Use ratios and rates for comparison.</p></li> 
    <li><p>Compute and interpret a rate of change.</p></li>
    <li><p>Interpret slope as a rate of change.</p></li>
    <li><p>Illustrate slope on a graph.</p></li>
    <li><p>Use the definition of slope to compute <m>\Delta y</m> or <m>\Delta x</m>.</p></li>
    <li><p>Recognize a linear relationship by its constant rate of change.</p></li>
    <li><p>Use formulas to compute slope.</p></li>
</ul></p></assemblage>

<activity xml:id="act-1-4-1"><title>Calculating Rate of Change</title>

<introduction><p>The graph shows how the thickness of a typical land-based glacier has changed over 43 years. 
    <image source="act-1-4-1.png" width="75%"><description>Graph of thickness on [1960,2040]x[0,100]</description></image></p></introduction>

<p><ol marker="a.">
    <li>At first glance, what does the graph tell you about the glaciers?</li>
    <li><p>What was the total change, <m>\Delta H</m>, in thickness from 1960 to 2003?</p>
    <tabular top="minor" halign="center" right="minor" left="minor" bottom="minor">              
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Year, <m>t</m></cell>
                    <cell>Thickness, <m>H</m></cell></row>
                <row><cell>1960</cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell></row>
                <row><cell>2003</cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell></row>
            </tabular>
            <p><m>~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\Delta H =</m></p>
    
            <aside component="instructor"><p>1b. Remind students that <m>\Delta H</m> can be negative. Also, explain the difference between total change and average yearly change.</p></aside></li>

    <li><p>Calculate the average yearly change in thickness, <m>\dfrac{\Delta H}{\Delta t}</m> over that time interval. Give units with your answers.
        <me>\dfrac{\Delta H}{\Delta t}=~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~</me></p></li>

    <li><sidebyside widths="50% 40%"><stack><p>The graph appears to be almost linear from 1992 to 2002. Read the graph to complete the table.</p>
       <tabular top="minor" halign="center" right="minor" left="minor" bottom="minor">              
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Year, <m>t</m></cell>
                    <cell>Thickness, <m>H</m></cell></row>
                <row><cell>1992</cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell></row>
                <row><cell>2002</cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell></row>
            </tabular></stack>
            <image source="act-1-4-2.png" width="75%"><description>Graph of thickness on [1992,2002]x[91,96]</description></image></sidebyside></li>

    <li><p>Calculate the slope of the graph from 1992 to 2002. Include units in your answer.</p></li>
    <li><p>Compare the average yearly change you calculated in part (c) with the slope you calculated in part (e). What do those values tell us about land-based glaciers?</p>
        <aside component="instructor"><p>Remind students that their answer should be a rate of change, with units.</p></aside></li>
</ol></p>
</activity>

<activity xml:id="act-1-4-3"><title>Interpreting Slope as a Rate</title>

<introduction><p>The amount of salt that can be dissolved in a beaker of water depends upon the temperature of the water. The table shows the amounts for several temperatures.</p>
  <tabular top="minor" halign="center" right="minor" left="minor" bottom="minor">              
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Temperature, <m>\degree</m>C, <m>T</m></cell>
                    <cell>10</cell>
                    <cell>12</cell>
                    <cell>15</cell>
                    <cell>21</cell>
                    <cell>25</cell>
                    <cell>40</cell>
                    <cell>52</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Salt, (g), <m>S</m></cell>
                    <cell>33</cell>
                    <cell>34</cell>
                    <cell>35.5</cell>
                    <cell>38.5</cell>
                    <cell>40.5</cell>
                    <cell>48</cell>
                    <cell>54</cell></row>
            </tabular></introduction>

<p><ol marker="a.">
    <li><p>Which variable is the input, and which is the output?</p>
            <p><m>\hphantom{00000}</m>input: <m>\hphantom{00000000000}</m>    output:  <m>\hphantom{00000}</m></p>                               
  <p>If you plot the data, which variable goes on the horizontal axis, and which on the vertical?</p>
            <p><m>\hphantom{00000}</m> horizontal: <m>\hphantom{00000000000}</m>   vertical:  <m>\hphantom{00000}</m></p></li>
    <li><p>Without plotting the points, how can you decide if they will lie on a straight line? Do that.</p>
            <aside component="instructor"><p>3b. Once students have thought of finding slopes, suggest that each person in their group calculate one slope.</p></aside></li>
    <li><p>Describe the slope of the line as a rate of change. Include units in your answer. What does the slope tell you about dissolving salt?</p></li>
    <li><p>Use the slope to answer the question: If you increase the temperature 5°C from the current temperature, how much more salt will dissolve?</p>
             <aside component="instructor"><p>3d. Many students will work out the answer using common sense. Show them how to use the definition of slope and solve an equation to get the same answer.</p></aside></li>
</ol></p>
</activity>

<activity xml:id="act-1-5-2"><title>Working with Formulas</title>

            <aside component="instructor"><p>Part(a) is intended to help students learn to work with <m>\Delta x</m>, <m>\Delta y</m>, and subscripted letters as ordinary variables. You might ask for volunteers to write their answers on the board.</p></aside>

<p><ol marker="a.">
    <li><p>We now have several new formulas to work with. Solve each formula for the indicated variable:</p>

        <p><m>m=\dfrac{\Delta y}{\Delta x} \hphantom{00000000000000} \Delta y =</m></p>

        <p><m>\Delta y = y_2 - y_1 \hphantom{00000000000}  y_2 = </m></p>

        <p><m>\Delta x = x_2 - x_1 \hphantom{00000000000}  x_2 = </m></p>

        <p><m>m = \dfrac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}  \hphantom{00000000000}  y_2-y_1 = </m></p></li>

    <li><p>Suppose you are studying the graph of a line, <m>y=mx+b</m>. Explain the difference between what the statements <m>x=2</m> and <m>\Delta x = 2</m> mean.</p>
            <aside component="instructor"><p>2b. Many students have trouble understanding the difference between <m>x</m> and <m>\Delta x</m>.</p></aside></li>

    <li><p>Explain how to find <m>f(x_1)</m> from a graph of <m>y=f(x)</m>.</p></li>
</ol></p>          
</activity>

<subsection><title>Checkpoint</title>
<p><ol>
    <li><p>What mathematical concept did you study in Activity 1?</p></li>
    <li><p>When you calculate slope, does the change in the input variable appear in the numerator or the denominator?</p></li>
    <li><p>What should you include in your calculation of slope to help you interpret it as a rate of change?</p></li>
    <li><p>Without graphing, how can you tell whether data points lie on a straight line?</p></li>
    <li><p>How does function notation appear in the formula for calculating slope?</p></li>
    </ol></p>
</subsection> 

<assemblage component="instructor"><title>Questions for Writing or Discussion</title>
<p><ol>
    <li><p>What do we use ratios and rates for?</p></li>
    <li><p>Suppose  represents your distance from home, in miles. Compare the meanings of the two statements <m>x=30</m> and <m>\Delta x=30</m>.</p></li>
    <li><p>Explain how slope measures the steepness of an incline.</p></li>
    <li><p>State two useful consequences of the fact that lines have constant slope.</p></li>
</ol></p></assemblage>

<assemblage component="instructor"><title>Concept Questions</title>
<p><ol>
    <li><p>Suppose <m>y</m> gives the weight in pounds of a bottle of water that holds <m>x</m> quarts.  What are the units of <m>\dfrac{\Delta y}{\Delta x}</m> ?</p>
        <p><ol marker="a.">
        <li>Water per bottle</li>    
        <li>Quarts per pound</li>
        <li>Pounds per quart</li>  
        <li>Quart-pounds</li>
    </ol></p></li>
    <li><p>What is wrong with the following reasoning? The point <m>(10,7)</m> lies on the graph of a line, so the slope of the line is <m>\dfrac{7}{10}</m>.</p>
        <p><ol marker="a.">
        <li>The slope is <m>\dfrac{10}{7}</m>.</li>    
        <li>7 is not an intercept.</li>
        <li>We need two points to compute the slope.</li>  
        <li>The line is decreasing.</li>
    </ol></p></li>
    <li><p>How can you tell from the equation of a line whether its graph is increasing or decreasing?</p>
        <p><ol marker="a.">
        <li>Its slope is positive.</li>    
        <li>Its <m>x</m>-intercept is positive.</li>
        <li>Its slope is negative.</li>  
        <li>Its <m>y</m>-intercept is negative.</li>
    </ol></p></li>
    <li><p>Comment on the following calculation:  The intercepts of a line are <m>(0,3)</m> and <m>(5,0)</m>, so the slope of the line is <m>\dfrac{3}{5}</m>.</p>
    <p><ol marker="a.">
        <li>It's true because we used two points.</li>    
        <li>It's false because <m>\Delta x</m> and <m>\Delta y</m> have opposite signs.</li>
        <li>It's true because the values are increasing.</li>  
        <li>It's false because the slope is <m>\dfrac{5}{3}</m>.</li>
    </ol></p></li>
</ol></p></assemblage>

</section>